LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Colloquium: Mostafa Beshkar
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Mostafa Beshkar is an Associate Professor of Economics a
 t Indiana University. His research interests center on International Trad
 e\, with a special emphasis on trade policy and institutions for internat
 ional trade cooperation. His articles have appeared in various journals i
 ncluding American Economic Journal-Microeconomics\, Journal of Internatio
 nal Economics\, Journal of Monetary Economics\, and International Economi
 c Review. He holds a PhD in Economics from Vanderbilt University.Title&#1
 60\;  Tariffs as Economic Statecraft: Evidence from the Hegemon-Periphery
  RelationshipAuthors: Mostafa Beshkar and Junho KimAbstract  Tariffs have
  long been understood as a tool of economic statecraft\, used to influenc
 e the behavior of trading partners. This may explain why trade agreements
  so often look lopsided: powerful countries grant weaker partners deeper 
 tariff cuts than they receive\, with the imbalance potentially compensate
 d through political alignment. Yet once signed\, agreements restrict a go
 vernment's ability to adjust tariffs actively for political ends. We hypo
 thesize that the threat of withdrawing concessions is nonetheless suffici
 ent to induce partners to align with the hegemon. To test this\, we explo
 it variation in implied concessions over time: as trade patterns shift\, 
 the concessions implied by a fixed tariff schedule are continuously reval
 ued\, even absent new tariff policy. Using the United States as the hegem
 on and UNGA voting to measure alignment\, we measure concessions as the t
 erms-of-trade effect of bilateral tariff cuts. We find that periods of la
 rger implied concessions are also periods of greater political alignment 
 by partner countries.About the Colloquium SeriesEach Monday throughout th
 e academic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research echoin
 g the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range from
  the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to the e
 nvironment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Presenters
  are from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zoom t
 o learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information\, s
 ee ourwebsite (https://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong><span data-contrast="none"><pictu
 re class="lw_image lw_image32327  lw_align_left lw_block"><source type="i
 mage/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/widt
 h/300/height/301/32327_bekshar_photo.rev.1788364587.webp 2x" data-origin=
 "responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/liv
 e/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/301/32327_bekshar_photo.rev.178
 8364587.jpg 2x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="300" height="301" a
 lt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/
 image/gid/105/width/300/height/301/32327_bekshar_photo.rev.1788364587.jpg
 " title="bekshar photo" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x
 /gid/105/width/300/height/301/32327_bekshar_photo.rev.1788364587.jpg 2x" 
 data-max-w="884" data-max-h="888" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/><
 /picture></span></strong></p><p><strong>Bio</strong></p><p>  Mostafa Besh
 kar is an Associate Professor of Economics at Indiana University. His res
 earch interests center on International Trade\, with a special emphasis o
 n trade policy and institutions for international trade cooperation. His 
 articles have appeared in various journals including American Economic Jo
 urnal-Microeconomics\, Journal of International Economics\, Journal of Mo
 netary Economics\, and International Economic Review. He holds a PhD in E
 conomics from Vanderbilt University.</p><p><strong>Title</strong>&#160\;<
 /p><p>  Tariffs as Economic Statecraft: Evidence from the Hegemon-Periphe
 ry Relationship</p><p><strong>Authors:</strong> Mostafa Beshkar and Junho
  Kim</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  Tariffs have long been under
 stood as a tool of economic statecraft\, used to influence the behavior o
 f trading partners. This may explain why trade agreements so often look l
 opsided: powerful countries grant weaker partners deeper tariff cuts than
  they receive\, with the imbalance potentially compensated through politi
 cal alignment. Yet once signed\, agreements restrict a government's abili
 ty to adjust tariffs actively for political ends. We hypothesize that the
  threat of withdrawing concessions is nonetheless sufficient to induce pa
 rtners to align with the hegemon. To test this\, we exploit variation in 
 implied concessions over time: as trade patterns shift\, the concessions 
 implied by a fixed tariff schedule are continuously revalued\, even absen
 t new tariff policy. Using the United States as the hegemon and UNGA voti
 ng to measure alignment\, we measure concessions as the terms-of-trade ef
 fect of bilateral tariff cuts. We find that periods of larger implied con
 cessions are also periods of greater political alignment by partner count
 ries.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="none">About the Colloquium Serie
 s</span></strong><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;134233117&quot\;:true\,&qu
 ot\;134233118&quot\;:true\,&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559685&qu
 ot\;:720\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:240}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast
 ="none">Each Monday throughout the academic year\, speakers share and dis
 cuss cutting edge research echoing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's re
 search program. Topics range from the governance of space\, cybersecurity
 \, and data management\, to the environment\, political economy\, and sus
 tainable food science. Presenters are from IU and around the world. Join 
 us at the workshop or over Zoom to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolvin
 g today. For more information\, see our</span><span data-contrast="none">
 <a href="https://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html" target="_blank" r
 el="noopener">website</a></span><span data-contrast="none">.</span></p>
