SUMMARY:Assessing the Effectiveness of Teaching Strategies in Large Classes
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Francesco Marchionne (Sr. Lecturer\, Business Econ
 omics)  At some point or other\, every teacher wonders about the effectiv
 eness of his or her pedagogical choices: did the learning activities actu
 ally make a difference or did students learn simply from repeated exposur
 e to the material? This question is perhaps even more pronounced in the l
 arge class setting\, where large enrollments make it difficult to track w
 hat students actually do.  In this first Kelley Teaching Seminar of the 2
 026-27 academic year\, Assurance of Learning Award winner Francesco March
 ionne will present his research\, which controls for the natural "learnin
 g curve bias" and provides real-time\, same-semester feedback that instru
 ctors can use to optimize instruction and maximize student learning\, eve
 n in large classes.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Presenter: Francesco Marchionne (Sr. Le
 cturer\, Business Economics)</p><p>  At some point or other\, every teach
 er wonders about the effectiveness of his or her pedagogical choices: did
  the learning activities actually make a difference or did students learn
  simply from repeated exposure to the material? This question is perhaps 
 even more pronounced in the large class setting\, where large enrollments
  make it difficult to track what students actually do.</p><p>  In this fi
 rst Kelley Teaching Seminar of the 2026-27 academic year\, Assurance of L
 earning Award winner Francesco Marchionne will present his research\, whi
 ch controls for the natural "learning curve bias" and provides real-time\
 , same-semester feedback that instructors can use to optimize instruction
  and maximize student learning\, even in large classes.</p>
