X-SOURCE:IU Kelley School of Business
DTSTART:20260922T200000Z
DTEND:20260922T211000Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:21JPSI Speaker Series: "Digital Transformation and Japan's Politi
 cal Economy" (Prof. Kay Shimizu\, University of Pittsburgh)
DESCRIPTION:This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\,
  and members of the community. Please join us!&#160\;  Professor Shimizu'
 s prepared remarks will be followed by a discussion/audience Q&amp\;A. GA
 /GISB 1118About the talk:In this public lecture\, Prof. Shimizu will disc
 uss lessons for businesses and governments from Japan's experience with "
 digital transformation." The digital transformation and demographic chang
 e are usually seen as two separate but equally threatening events that fo
 reshadow job replacement\, industrial decline and social bifurcation. Bec
 ause Japan is the world's frontrunner in demographic change with an agein
 g and shrinking society\, it is facing these two disruptions at the exact
  same time. This creates a "lucky moment"\, as it presents an opportunity
  to employ one as a solution for the problems caused by the other. For ex
 ample\, Japan's traditional sectors are replaced by digital systems that 
 demand fewer people while offering new jobs. Emerging technologies are op
 ening fresh opportunities for Japanese companies to compete globally. The
  twin disruptions are also upending Japan's political economy. As compani
 es reinvent business strategies and employees reskill to pursue individua
 l careers\, the state is reorganizing to find a new role in balancing the
  unfolding demands of the digital economy.This event is co-sponsored by t
 he Cybersecurity and Global Policy Program and Department of Political Sc
 ience.About the speaker:Kay Shimizuis a Research Professor in the Graduat
 e School of International and Public Affairs at the University of Pittsbu
 rgh. She earned her B.A. in Economics and International Relations and her
  Ph.D. in Political Science from Stanford University. Her research addres
 ses institutional design and their effects on economic governance with a 
 focus on East Asia. She is the author of Betting on the Farm: Institution
 al Change in Japanese Agriculture (with Patricia Maclachlan)\, and The Di
 gital Transformation and Japan's Political Economy (with Ulrike Schaede).
  Her current research is about the confluence of demographic change and t
 he digital transformation in the East Asian context.This event is made po
 ssible thanks to generous financial support from the Japan Foundation. Al
 l 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unless otherwise noted.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This public event is open to all studen
 ts\, faculty\, staff\, and members of the community. Please join us!&#160
 \;</p><p>  Professor Shimizu's prepared remarks will be followed by a dis
 cussion/audience Q&amp\;A. <strong>GA/GISB 1118</strong></p><p><span styl
 e="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>About the talk</strong>:</span><
 /p><p><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off fon
 t-feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none">In this 
 public lecture\, Prof. Shimizu will discuss lessons for businesses and go
 vernments from Japan's experience with "digital transformation."</span> T
 he digital transformation and demographic change are usually seen as two 
 separate but equally threatening events that foreshadow job replacement\,
  industrial decline and social bifurcation. Because Japan is the world's 
 frontrunner in demographic change with an ageing and shrinking society\, 
 it is facing these two disruptions at the exact same time. This creates a
  "lucky moment"\, as it presents an opportunity to employ one as a soluti
 on for the problems caused by the other. For example\, Japan's traditiona
 l sectors are replaced by digital systems that demand fewer people while 
 offering new jobs. Emerging technologies are opening fresh opportunities 
 for Japanese companies to compete globally. The twin disruptions are also
  upending Japan's political economy. As companies reinvent business strat
 egies and employees reskill to pursue individual careers\, the state is r
 eorganizing to find a new role in balancing the unfolding demands of the 
 digital economy.</p><p><em>This event is co-sponsored by the Cybersecurit
 y and Global Policy Program and Department of Political Science.</em></p>
 <div><strong style="font-size: 16px\;"><span style="text-decoration: unde
 rline\;">About the speaker:</span></strong></div><div class="c-vertical-t
 abs" data-ms-component="vertical-tabs" data-vertical-tabs-display="vertic
 al"><div class="c-vertical-tabs__item" data-vertical-tabs-component="tab"
  data-vertical-tabs-state="is-open"><div class="c-vertical-tabs__content 
 vertical-tabs__content-long open" data-vertical-tabs-component="content" 
 aria-hidden="false" id="collapsible-1" role="region"><div class="s-wysiwy
 g"><div class="s-wysiwyg"><strong style="font-size: 16px\;">Kay Shimizu</
 strong><span style="font-size: 16px\;">is a Research Professor in the Gra
 duate School of International and Public Affairs at the University of Pit
 tsburgh. She earned her B.A. in Economics and International Relations and
  her Ph.D. in Political Science from Stanford University. Her research ad
 dresses institutional design and their effects on economic governance wit
 h a focus on East Asia. She is the author of Betting on the Farm: Institu
 tional Change in Japanese Agriculture (with Patricia Maclachlan)\, and Th
 e Digital Transformation and Japan's Political Economy (with Ulrike Schae
 de). Her current research is about the confluence of demographic change a
 nd the digital transformation in the East Asian context.</span></div><div
  class="s-wysiwyg"></div><div class="s-wysiwyg"><span style="font-size: 1
 6px\;"><em>This event is made possible thanks to generous financial suppo
 rt from the Japan Foundation. All 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unless
  otherwise noted.</em></span></div></div></div></div></div>
