SUMMARY:Virtual Workshop: Polycentric Public Insurance and Climate Adapta
 tion in Switzerland
DESCRIPTION:As climate losses mount and private insurers retreat from vul
 nerable areas worldwide\, the questions of who bears the risk and who act
 s on adaptation are becoming urgent. This talk presents findings from a s
 tudy\, conducted with Rebecca Bednarek and Konstantinos Chalkias\, explor
 ing how Switzerland's nineteen cantonal public-sector disaster insurers c
 an provide compulsory\, affordable\, multiperil coverage without governme
 nt subsidy while actively investing in prevention and mitigation. We prop
 ose that mechanisms familiar to Ostrom scholars — reciprocity\, polycen
 tricity\, and self-regulation — can be extended to explain how governme
 nt actors achieve what cross-sectoral partnerships often cannot\, challen
 ging the dominant view that the public sector should steer rather than ro
 w in addressing grand challenges.Eugenia Cacciatori is Reader (Senior Ass
 ociate Professor) in Management at Bayes Business School\, City St George
 's\, University of London\, researching technological and organizational 
 innovation\, professional knowledge\, and the governance of complex socie
 tal challenges. Her recent research explores disaster insurance and AI ad
 option. Her work appears in journals such as the Academy of Management Jo
 urnal\, Organization Studies\, and Research Policy.Paula Jarzabkowski is 
 Professor of Strategic Management at the University of Queensland and Bay
 es Business School. A Fellow of the British Academy and of the Academy of
  the Social Sciences in Australia\, she is a leading authority on the ins
 urance protection gap and disaster resilience\, and serves on the OECD Hi
 gh-Level Advisory Board for the Financial Management of Catastrophic Risk
 . She received the Academy of Management's Joanne Martin Trailblazer Awar
 d for her foundational work on Strategy-as-Practice.  —  This colloquiu
 m will start with 20 minutes of introductory remarks\, followed by a disc
 ussion moderated by Greg Bloom\, visiting scholar at the Ostrom Workshop 
 and co-founder of the Climate Resilient Insurance Strategy Project (CRISP
 ).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><picture class="lw_image lw_image32149  l
 w_align_left lw_block"><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type="image/web
 p" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/500/height/273/
 32149_CJ_snip.rev.1787580110.webp 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><source m
 edia="(max-width: 500px)" type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu
 /live/image/gid/105/width/500/height/273/32149_CJ_snip.rev.1787580110.jpg
  1x" data-origin="responsive"/><source media="(min-width: 501px)" type="i
 mage/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/609/hei
 ght/333/32149_CJ_snip.rev.1787580110.webp 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><
 source media="(min-width: 501px)" type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://event
 s.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/609/height/333/32149_CJ_snip.rev.178758
 0110.jpg 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="609" height="333" alt=
 "" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/ima
 ge/gid/105/width/609/height/333/32149_CJ_snip.rev.1787580110.jpg" title="
 CJ snip" data-max-w="827" data-max-h="452" loading="lazy" data-optimized=
 "true"/></picture></p><p>  As climate losses mount and private insurers r
 etreat from vulnerable areas worldwide\, the questions of who bears the r
 isk and who acts on adaptation are becoming urgent. This talk presents fi
 ndings from a study\, conducted with Rebecca Bednarek and Konstantinos Ch
 alkias\, exploring how Switzerland's nineteen cantonal public-sector disa
 ster insurers can provide compulsory\, affordable\, multiperil coverage w
 ithout government subsidy while actively investing in prevention and miti
 gation. We propose that mechanisms familiar to Ostrom scholars — recipr
 ocity\, polycentricity\, and self-regulation — can be extended to expla
 in how government actors achieve what cross-sectoral partnerships often c
 annot\, challenging the dominant view that the public sector should steer
  rather than row in addressing grand challenges.</p><p><strong>Eugenia Ca
 cciatori</strong> is Reader (Senior Associate Professor) in Management at
  Bayes Business School\, City St George's\, University of London\, resear
 ching technological and organizational innovation\, professional knowledg
 e\, and the governance of complex societal challenges. Her recent researc
 h explores disaster insurance and AI adoption. Her work appears in journa
 ls such as the <em>Academy of Management Journal</em>\, <em>Organization 
 Studies</em>\, and <em>Research Policy</em>.</p><p><strong>Paula Jarzabko
 wski</strong> is Professor of Strategic Management at the University of Q
 ueensland and Bayes Business School. A Fellow of the British Academy and 
 of the Academy of the Social Sciences in Australia\, she is a leading aut
 hority on the insurance protection gap and disaster resilience\, and serv
 es on the OECD High-Level Advisory Board for the Financial Management of 
 Catastrophic Risk. She received the Academy of Management's Joanne Martin
  Trailblazer Award for her foundational work on Strategy-as-Practice.</p>
 <p>  —</p><p>  This colloquium will start with 20 minutes of introducto
 ry remarks\, followed by a discussion moderated by Greg Bloom\, visiting 
 scholar at the Ostrom Workshop and co-founder of the Climate Resilient In
 surance Strategy Project (CRISP).</p>
